    {
      "id": "formate_asym_1585",
      "species": "Formate*",
      "group": "formate",
      "pair": 7,
      "vibration": {
        "category": "stretch",
        "subtype": "asymmetric",
        "branch": null
      },
      "atoms": "O-C-O",
      "wn_start": 1515,
      "wn_end": 1620,
      "short": "νₐₛ(OCO) HCOO*",
      "description": "Formate asymmetric OCO stretch (HCOO*); key HCOO* marker. Position is support-dependent: ~1520 cm⁻¹ on Ru/Al₂O₃ and Ru–Na₂O/Al₂O₃, shifting to ~1585–1600 cm⁻¹ on oxide-rich surfaces (Al₂O₃, CuGaZrOx). Appears during H₂ reduction as a methanation intermediate formed at the metal–support interface.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "key": "Proano.InsituDRIFTSStudy.2019",
          "wn": 1520,
          "site": ["Ru/Al₂O₃", "Ru/\"Na₂O\"/Al₂O₃"],
          "note": "Table 2/Fig. 1a (Ru/Al₂O₃) and Fig. 3a (Ru–6.1%\"Na₂O\"/Al₂O₃ DFM): νas(OCO) HCOO* at 1520 cm⁻¹ on both surfaces, appearing at the onset of H₂ reduction (methanation step, 320 °C, 20% H₂/N₂). On Ru/Al₂O₃, formate forms via spillover of bicarbonates (adsorbed on Al₂O₃ OH groups); on Ru–Na₂O/Al₂O₃, it forms after bidentate carbonates (on Al-O⁻-Na⁺ sites) spill over to the Ru–support interface — same position despite the different CO₂ adsorption pathway. Confirmed reaction intermediate en route to CH₄."
        },
        {
          "key": "AlAbdulghani.UncoveringPressureDependentMechanism.2025",
          "wn": 1600,
          "site": "CuGaZrOx",
          "note": "νas(OCO) at 1600 cm⁻¹ (ME-DRIFTS, 20 bar, 260°C); confirmed active intermediate by CO₂ modulation-excitation PSD — responds at same frequency as product formation. Also at 1590 cm⁻¹ during methanol steam reforming on Cu-GaZrOx-24."
        },
        {
          "key": "Rajput.InsituDRIFTSKineticthermodynamic.2025",
          "wn": 1585,
          "site": "Al₂O₃",
          "note": "Table 3: νas(OCO⁻) at 1585 cm⁻¹; dominant CH₄ adsorption product on Al₂O₃ via H-abstraction; nearly absent on La-modified and Ni-Ce-Fe/La-Al₂O₃ under DRM."
        },
        {
          "key": "Noei.RevealingFormicAcid.2026",
          "site": ["Fe₃O₄(001)", "Fe₃O₄(111)"],
          "note": "Table 1, Fig. 6b–d: νas(OCO) of bidentate HCOO* resolved by p-polarized IRRAS (Fano line shape, UHV, room temperature). On Fe₃O₄(001) (Fig. 6c): two bidentate-bridging components, 1555 cm⁻¹ (tet site: oct-Fe above tet-Fe beneath) and 1548/1538 cm⁻¹ (p/s-pol; int site: oct-Fe above interstitial Fe) — paired νs(OCO) at 1369/1377 cm⁻¹ (tet/int); distinction only possible with polarized light due to Fano overlap in unpolarized spectra. On Fe₃O₄(111) (Fig. 6b/d): quasi-bidentate at 1588 cm⁻¹ (one O to Fe-tet, other to surface OH; paired νs at 1335 cm⁻¹, positive inverted Fano sign — TDM parallel to surface) and chelating at 1548 cm⁻¹ very weak (both O to single Fe-tet; paired νs at 1380 cm⁻¹); bidentate-bridging excluded here (Fe-tet neighbor distance 5.9 Å too large). Chelating forms first (from 0.05 L); quasi-bidentate develops above 0.2 L."
        }
      ],
      "intensity": "s",
      "width": "medium",
      "confidence": "confirmed",
      "tags": ["site-sensitive"]
    },
